 \subsection{LLM-Based Seed Generation}
 Recent work has explored LLMs for generating or enriching testing inputs.
 Fuzz4All~\cite{xia2024fuzz4all} uses LLMs as a general input generator, and ShQveL~\cite{zhong2025testing} synthesizes SQL fragments for database testing.
-SmartFuzz~\cite{lin2026smartfuzz} uses LLMs to synthesize new seeds from features extracted from official DBMS documentation and historical bug-triggering inputs, targeting bug-prone execution states.
-In contrast, \tool{} transfers existing test cases from mature DBMSs, which cover a broad range of SQL features, and preserves their cross-statement structures.
-The two approaches therefore provide complementary seeds: SmartFuzz targets bug-prone features, whereas \tool{} provides broad feature coverage, and they could be combined to construct initial seeds for DBMS fuzzing.
+SmartFuzz~\cite{lin2026smartfuzz} uses LLMs to generate new DBMS-specific seeds from features extracted from official DBMS documentation and historical bug-triggering inputs.
+In contrast, \tool{} transfers existing test cases across DBMSs and uses cross-statement dependencies and cumulative schema and type context for translation and repair.
+The two approaches construct seeds from different sources and can be combined to construct initial seeds for DBMS fuzzing.
 \sedar{}~\cite{fu2024sedar} is the closest work to \tool{} because it also transfers existing DBMS test cases into fuzzing seeds; its compatibility mechanism is described in Section~\ref{sec:background}.
 
 Unlike \sedar{}, which translates each statement of a test case individually under its referenced source-side sub-schema, \tool{} performs AQT-guided instantiation that maintains target-side context across statements, where explicit dependencies, nested structures, and clause-level relationships support local repair with parser and execution feedback before mutation.
